        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitkeweenaw.com/places-to-visit/hancock/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city is named after John Hancock, a signer of the Declaration of Independence.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hancock%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Jack Stevens Rail Trail, built on an abandoned Soo Line grade, winds through Hancock and continues 14 miles north to Calumet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hancock%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The historic Scott Hotel, built in 1906, stands beside the former Kerredge Theater and has hosted many Upper Peninsula events.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hancock%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Hancock’s downtown houses the Finnish-American Heritage Centre, the largest collection of Finnish-American history and a Keweenaw Heritage Site.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
